#open-question(title: [Is the Grip Deficit Explained by Muscle Mass, Deconditioning, or Disease?])[
A low grip force has an obvious alternative explanation: less muscle. Three lines of evidence bear on whether that explanation is sufficient.

*The muscle-mass explanation.* In long COVID, appendicular lean mass index explained 52% of the association for grip strength and 39% for leg extension @RamirezVelez2023ALMI — a substantial share, and the authors read the deficit as largely muscle-mass-mediated (sarcopenic). In fibromyalgia, grip strength was an independent predictor of vastus lateralis cross-sectional area, and the regression model explained 46.6% of the variance in muscle size @FernandezPola2026architecture — strength and muscle size co-vary rather than dissociate. Neither study supports a large disease-specific residual in strength after accounting for muscle size.

*What is left over, and what is not known.* Roughly half of the long-COVID grip association remained unexplained by lean mass, and the muscle-architecture study did not test strength as an outcome at all. Neither study was performed in ME/CFS. No included ME/CFS study adjusted for body composition, and none matched habitual activity between groups before comparing grip force. The residual deficit in ME/CFS is therefore unknown rather than established — which is the open question, not evidence against deconditioning.

*The comparator argument.* Three comparator conditions can separate the explanations: myasthenia gravis (fatigability with normal resting strength, immune neuromuscular junction), cancer-related fatigue (deconditioning-matched), and sedentary healthy controls matched for step count. If ME/CFS shows a recovery-ratio deficit that myasthenia does not show at the same absolute force, the deficit is not simple weakness.

One of the three comparisons has already been reported, and it cuts against disease specificity: the adult cohort included 18 patients with cancer-related fatigue, and in women that group showed the same higher fatigue ratios and reduced recovery ratios as the ME/CFS group @Jakel2021HGS. The remaining comparisons — myasthenia gravis and activity-matched controls — have not been performed, and no included study adjusted for muscle mass or activity level. The comparator question and the muscle-mass question are the same question asked from two directions: how much of the grip deficit is left when you subtract everything that is not ME/CFS?

*Prediction:* after adjustment for appendicular lean mass and habitual activity, the ME/CFS recovery-ratio deficit persists, while the absolute-force difference attenuates toward null. *Falsified if:* adjustment for muscle mass and activity removes the group difference on every index.

*Certainty: 0.30.* (Indirect, cross-condition support; no ME/CFS study with body-composition adjustment.)

*Consequence:* This is the difference between "your muscles are weak because you have been ill and inactive" and "something about the illness itself changes how muscle recovers" — the first is used to justify graded exercise, the second changes what clinicians should look for.
] <oq:handgrip-deconditioning-vs-disease>
